<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Free France</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1944 CE · capital London; later Algiers</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Charles de Gaulle's government-in-exile and armed forces, which kept France in the war against Germany after 1940 and rallied much of the empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 18 June 1940 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, refusing the armistice, broadcast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on the BBC calling on the French to continue fighting. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> recognised him as leader of the Free French, and in August 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, under its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-eboue">Félix Éboué</a>, joined his cause, followed by most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built an army and navy, fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bir-hakeim">Bir Hakeim</a> in 1942 and gathered colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Pacific. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-national-committee">French National Committee</a> was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">De Gaulle's</a> envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-moulin">Jean Moulin</a> united the internal Resistance in 1943. That year the movement moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud's</a> forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In June 1944 the committee became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-french-republic">Provisional Government of the French Republic</a>, which returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> after its liberation in August 1944 and restored the Republic.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-france.jpg" alt="Free France" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Free_France_(1940-1944).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>London; later Algiers</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles de Gaulle, Félix Éboué, Jean Moulin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free French, Fighting France</dd></dl></aside></div>
